 { Welcome to this chapter, where we'll be dealing with an important positional concept: space! Space refers to the total number of squares controlled by each side on the chess board. It is a tricky topic of study and one that confuses many beginner to intermediate players. Not helpful in this respect is that players will often hear the term 'space advantage' thrown around. And if you had no idea about the game of chess you would probably assume that this description speaks to the fact that more space results in a positional advantage. This, however, is not true! At least not always. In general, controlling a greater portion of the board than your opponent will be an asset. But in truth the real key lies in the effect that space has on piece mobility. When one side controls more of the board they usually enjoy access to better squares for their pieces, while the opponent can easily find themselves being suffocated without good options for their own army - particularly so the minor pieces. This is why more space is usually an advantage. However, you can and will get situations frequently where you may have more space but your opponent is the one with the better squares for their pieces. This is a tricky concept to explain with words alone, so we will be using the current position to show what is meant by this now. } 10... { -KEY- } d4 $2 { Black pushes the pawns forward and in so doing they 'grab space'. After all, their d4 pawn is the only one in the board that has crossed into enemy territory. It turns out however that it is White that has the much better position. Play would continue: } 11. b4 $1 { Expanding on the queenside. } a5 { Fighting for space on the queenside, and seeking to provoke the pawn advance in order to loosen White's control over the c5 square. } ( 11... f5 $2 { Black does not benefit from making a similar kingside advance, as now the e6 square becomes chronically weak while the e5 pawn can no longer be defended by a pawn. } 12. c5 { White's queenside advantage is much more significant than Black's, and importantly the advance of the c-pawn means that White will be able to use the c4 square for their knight soon. } Kh8 { Black tries to bring the king to a safer square. } 13. Nc4 Qc7 14. Nxd4 $18 { Unsurprisingly, the pressure results in a tactical opportunity for White, winning a pawn due to the pin against the knight on e7. We can see from this variation that Black faced a couple of typical problems when expanding pawns: 1. The central pawn formation was subject to huge pressure from White's pieces. 2. There were too many vital weak squares created as a result of the expansion. Notice that while White also left weaknesses with their queenside advance (namely the outpost on c3) Black was not able to make use of this. } ) 
12. b5 $16 { Suddenly, we can see that White's queenside space advantage is much more meaningful than Black's advanced central pawn chain. The problem that Black faces is that their structure does not impede White's minor pieces from becoming active. We can see that White's fianchettoed bishop on g2 is a very effective piece, while their f3 knight is pressuring Black's central pawns. The White knight on d2 can pivot to either e4 or b3 depending on what Black does, while their dark-squared bishop eyes up the a3 square. On the other hand, Black's minor pieces are all rather passive, with both of their bishops hemmed in by pieces or pawns. } Nc5 { Black finally finds one powerful square for a minor piece, so they immediately use it to pressure the base of the White pawn chain. } 13. Nb3 $1 { Immediately forcing off Black's only active piece. } Nxb3 ( 13... Na4 $6 { Black does not have time to go hunting for the c3 outpost. For example: } 14. Ba3 Nc3 15. Qd2 f6 { The only way to defend the pawn, but it allows a deadly tactic. } 16. Nfxd4 { The c3 outpost is dead, and Black is completely lost. } exd4 17. Bxe7 $18 { winning more material. } ) 
14. axb3 { If we consider the space situation in a purely optical sense, we can see that White has far superior control on the queenside, but it would appear that Black is doing better in the centre. However, White's structure is far more stable since Black's e5 and b7 pawns are quite loose. Importantly, all of White's minor pieces have greater potential than their Black counterparts. For these reasons, White is already winning. } f6 { Black tries to stabilise the central pawn chain. } 15. b4 $1 { Not the only move, but it makes sense to use the pin against the a8 rook to keep making progress on the queenside. } Be6 { This move loses material, but there were no better options. } 16. bxa5 Rxa5 17. Rxa5 Qxa5 18. Nxd4 $18 { White secures a pawn while keeping the other advantages in their position such as the pressure against the queenside. We can see that even though in the starting position the optics looked to be in Black's favour, the squares under White's control were the more valuable ones as they were able to offer White a tremendous amount of mobility and activity The most important takeaway of the introduction is that space is usually, but not always, an advantage. What truly matters is whether the extra board control provides tangible benefits to that side's piece activity. We'll now begin our study of the three sections of this chapter, which will focus on the following aspects: 1. How to evaluate space 2. The space advantage 3. Fighting against space. Let's dive in! } 
